WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:04.980
Imagine we listed all
integers in a growing spiral

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.840
and colored the
prime numbers blue

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.350
and left the composite
numbers black.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.840
One interesting
question we may ask

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.450
is how many primes are there
compared to composites?

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:20.610
First, let's zoom out
to see the big picture.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:24.250
Notice the prime color is
dense in the center and slowly

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:28.040
drops off in the distance,
but never seems to end.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.970
One way I like to think
about this is as follows.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.060
Imagine there is a tree
at the center, which

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.140
is infinitely high.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.970
The leaves which
drop from this tree

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:40.700
represent prime numbers, which
are scattered unpredictably

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.940
below, dense near
the base of the tree.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.790
And as we walk away
from this tree,

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:51.630
we find fewer leaves,
though we always find them.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.320
This is exactly
what happens when

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.840
we look at larger
and larger integers.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.210
We always find more primes,
though the number of primes

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.470
we find gradually drops
the further we look.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.350
So let's return to our question.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:10.280
How many primes are there
less than some integer x?

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.790
If we make a table, we
see the number of primes

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.920
is always increasing.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.580
though as we search further,
we find fewer and fewer.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.180
Let's graph the number of primes
found on the vertical axis

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.270
and the search size
x on the horizontal.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:28.350
As we zoom out to include
billions of numbers,

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.980
notice the curve
never flatlines.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:35.920
It's always rising,
albeit gradually.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.880
First, let's think about
the density of primes

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.870
less than some integer x.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:43.040
We can find the
density by dividing

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.150
the number of primes
found by the search size.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:50.330
For the first 100 integers,
we find 25 primes.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.900
Therefore, 25% are prime.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.840
Of the first 10,000 integers,
we find 1,229 primes.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.500
12.29% are prime.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.490
Of the first 1 million
integers, 7.84% are prime.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:12.330
And the first 100 million
integers contain 5.76% prime.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.070
As we search further, this
density continues to drop,

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:20.162
though the speed at which
it drops slows down.

00:02:20.162 --> 00:02:23.980
Here is a graph of the search
size on the horizontal axis

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.470
and the prime density
on the vertical.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.640
Notice that as we
zoom out, the primes

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.975
are a vanishing proportion
of all integers.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.670
Amazingly, we find
this formula in nature.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:45.080
We see it in galaxies,
storms, flowers,

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:49.950
and even inside our bodies as
the design of least resistance,

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.850
known as the logarithmic spiral.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.300
Notice that as the
spiral rotates,

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.870
it gets further and further
away from the center.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.650
Incredibly, the rotation
rate of a logarithmic spiral

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:07.270
is related to the density
of primes as follows.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.540
We have the number of rotations.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.140
Call this phi.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.730
And the distance from
the center, call this r.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.550
If we graph phi
against r and zoom out,

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.330
we see they are
related according

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:24.550
to the natural logarithm.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:28.100
This means the natural
logarithm of the distance

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.780
is related to the
number of rotations.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.000
The graph of the
natural logarithm

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.380
is commonly written using
the variable names y and x,

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:40.360
as y equals the
natural logarithm of x.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.190
Notice the graph tapers
off in the same way

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.320
the density of primes
gradually decreases.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:52.470
The final step is to invert
this by changing the y-axis to 1

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:55.630
divided by the natural
logarithm of x.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:59.440
And when we zoom out, we find
the exact same curve generated

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:02.300
when we plot the
density of primes.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.270
Let's confirm this by
overlapping the two plots.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:08.600
In green is a graph
of the line y equals 1

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.450
over the natural logarithm of x.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:15.920
And in red is the plot of
prime number density up to x.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.640
As we zoom out, they
approach each other.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.950
The further we zoom
out, the more accurate

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.240
the green estimate becomes.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.710
This is known as
the asymptotic law

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.940
of distribution
of prime numbers.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:30.860
We now have a formula
to accurately tell us

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.355
the density of primes
without counting.

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:41.410
The density of primes up to some
integer x is approximately 1

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:45.270
divided by the natural
logarithm of x, or ln(x).

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.980
So let's say you need to
know the density of primes

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.460
between one and 100 trillion.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:51.160
Simple.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:57.630
1 divided by ln of 100
trillion equals 3.1%.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.350
Compare this to
the actual result

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:04.660
from counting all
primes, which is 3.2%.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:09.060
This is off by 0.1%, and as
we check larger and larger

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.290
numbers, the difference
approaches 0.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:16.030
Realize now that we can use
this formula for prime density

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:19.420
to estimate the number
of primes up to x.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:22.470
The number of primes is the
area under the density curve

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:26.050
for which we can simplify by
assuming density is constant.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:30.170
So number of primes
equals size times density,

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:33.600
or x divided by ln(x).

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.580
This is the prime
number theorem.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:41.850
Here is a graph of y equals
x divided by ln(x) in blue.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.910
And in yellow is a plot of
an actual count of primes.

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:48.360
Notice as we zoom out,
these lines eventually

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.570
overlap as we look to infinity.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:53.600
And that is it.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.150
We have a formula which tells
us approximately how many primes

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:00.817
there are up to any
value, no counting needed.

00:06:00.817 --> 00:06:02.400
For example, let's
say we need to know

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.250
the number of primes
less than 100 trillion.

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:09.240
100 trillion divided by the
natural log of 100 trillion

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.270
equals 3.1 trillion.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:15.730
Compare this to the actual
count, which is 3.2 trillion.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:19.540
This is over 99.99%
accurate, even

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.400
at this relatively small scale.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:26.720
So to recap, given a search
size up to some integer x,

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.820
the prime density is
about 1 divided by ln(x).

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:36.830
And the number of primes is
about x divided by ln(x).

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.640
This is the prime
number theorem.

